From 6491bf8f47da39da565b36c06b87914b2fb597bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vroom <vroom@localhost>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:21:35 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] kconfig: evaluate $(shell,...) speculatively in parallel

Kconfig runs every $(shell,...) through popen() one at a time; an arm64
LLVM config runs ~240 commands (compiler/assembler/linker probes, each a
/bin/sh plus mkdir/cc/rm) and syncconfig spends ~4 s waiting on them.

On a cache miss, fork() a child that inherits the complete parser state
and continues the parse in "record" mode: $(shell,...) commands are not
run but written to a pipe.  The parent runs recorded commands in
parallel (one per CPU), keyed by exact command text, and resumes as soon
as the result it needs is available, so parsing and probing overlap.
Commands whose text depends on an earlier result are mispredicted, miss
again and start another recorder; recording children use known results,
so each round predicts exactly.  stderr is captured per command and
replayed when the result is consumed.

Every consumed result comes from running exactly the same command text
with the same /bin/sh and environment, so the generated .config,
auto.conf, autoconf.h and auto.conf.cmd are byte-identical to serial
evaluation.  The child gets private file offsets for the open Kconfig
files so its read-ahead cannot move the parent's.

KCONFIG_SHELL_JOBS=N limits parallelism (1 disables prefetching);
KCONFIG_SHELL_DEBUG=1 prints statistics.

syncconfig, conf only, M1 Pro: 4.1 s -> 1.0 s.
---
 scripts/kconfig/parser.y     |   1 +
 scripts/kconfig/preprocess.c | 564 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 scripts/kconfig/preprocess.h |   1 +
 3 files changed, 553 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

